Pedestrian Detection Method Based on Fusion of LIDAR and Camera
Yangyang Fan1,2, Yuansheng Liu1,2* 1Beijing Key Laboratory of Information Service Engineering, Beijing Union University, Beijing 100101, China 2College of Robotics, Beijing Union University, Beijing 100101, China 20231083510922@buu.edu.cn, yuansheng@buu.edu.cn *corresponding author
Abstract—As two important sensors in the perception process of autonomous driving, LIDAR is weak in judging target details, and camera typically have more demanding requirements regarding light conditions. In order to overcome the limitations of single sensor in pedestrian detection, a pedestrian detection method based on the fusion of laser radar and camera is proposed.Firstly, the M1 solid-state LIDAR is temporally and spatially synchronised with the camera, and then the point cloud is processed to achieve point cloud downsampling and ground point cloud removal and the processed point cloud is clustered using the density-based DBSCAN clustering algorithm. Concurrently, the camera image is used to detect pedestrians using the YOLOv5 visual target detection algorithm. Finally, the detection results of the two sensors are fused by decision level fusion to obtain the final detection information. The experiments carried out in the campus scene confirm that the accuracy of the proposed method for pedestrian fusion detection is 85.7%, and it solves the problem of missed detection of visual detection caused by light factors of camera sensors.
Keywords-Autonomous driving perception; M1 solid-state LIDAR; Camera; Pedestrian detection; Decision level fusion
1. INTRODUCTION
With the rapid development of automatic driving technology, target detection technology has become one of its core technologies[1]. In complex traffic environments, accurately and quickly identifying and locating various targets is the key to ensure the safe and stable operation of autonomous driving systems[2-4]. As two complementary sensors, LIDAR and camera play important roles in target detection tasks with their unique advantages, respectively[5]. LIDAR can generate high-precision point clouds at a longer range due to the high energy of laser pulses[6], and can maintain stable operation under various kinds of bad weather, while cameras can provide rich color and texture information. However, a single sensor often struggles to handle all scenarios. Therefore, effectively integrating the data from LIDAR and camera has become an effective approach to enhance the performance of target detection[7]. For example, Nobis[8] et al proposed a deep learning-based camera and LIDAR fusion framework, which achieves effective fusion of multimodal data by jointly training a convolutional
neural network and a point cloud processing network. The PointPainting+ algorithm proposed by Shen Caiying[9] et al effectively improves the problem of poor detection of long-distance small targets such as pedestrians and cyclists due to the sparsity of point clouds. Tingting Liang[10] et al, proposed the BEVFusion framework, which uses two independent processes to process LIDAR and camera data, and then fuses them at the bird's-eye view level, addressing the limitations of existing methods that overly rely on LIDAR. However, although the existing research has achieved certain results, target detection by LIDAR and camera fusion still faces many challenges in terms of real-time and stability. In this context, We not only proposes a pedestrian detection method by fusion of LIDAR and camera, but also adopts a high-performance M1 solid-state LIDAR in the experimental part, which is capable of capturing more details than other LIDARS, and has a better perception of the target in order to identify the target more accurately. The algorithm firstly achieves coordinate conversion between the LIDAR and camera sensors through an accurate joint calibration process to ensure data consistency. Then, the deep learning YOLOv5 model is used as the visual detection algorithm, and the density-based DBSCAN algorithm is used to cluster the preprocessed point cloud targets. The detection results from both sensors are integrated through a decision-level fusion approach, ultimately obtaining information for pedestrian detection. Finally, experiments are carried out in the campus environment. The results show that the proposed method can accurately fuse pedestrian information, while significantly improving the visual detection leakage problem of camera sensors due to lighting factors.
2. RELATED WORK
2.1. Sensor Fusion Strategy Classification
In the environmental perception process of autonomous vehicles, to overcome the limitations of a single sensor in terms of sensing capability, sensor fusion technology is often used at present, which can be classified into data-level fusion, feature-level fusion and decision-level fusion according to the different stages of data processing in which fusion occurs[11]. Data-level fusion, also known as pixel-level fusion, is a way of low-level data fusion. It directly correlates and calibrates the raw data collected by each sensor, and then performs the fusion process to extract features from the fused data, which ultimately results in
