2.2. Target Detection based on sensor fusion
At present, most of the target detection methods based on LIDAR and camera fusion use feature-level fusion and decision-level fusion. Algorithms that use feature-level fusion are MV3D-Net[12], AVOD-Net[13], PointPainting[14], and others. Those that use decision-level fusion encompass methods such as the Dempster-Shafer theory[15] and weighted fusion[16]. The MV3D-Net algorithm fuses LIDAR point cloud information and visual images, and generates 3D candidate regions on the top view and front view of LIDAR to achieve accurate target detection. However, its detection time is too long and the network structure is complex to meet the real-time performance. To solve the above problems, Jason Ku et al. proposed the AVOD-Net algorithm. As an improvement of MV3D-Net, AVOD-Net not only reduces the number of parameters, but also improves the detection speed by removing the intensity information in the front view input and top view of the LIDAR and adding the FPN network to extract features. The PointPainting algorithm maps the semantic segmentation results of the image to the point cloud, and uses the rich color and texture information of the image to enhance the feature representation of the point cloud, which not only improves the semantic
understanding ability of the point cloud, but also achieves higher accuracy in the 3D target detection task. Dempster-Shafer theory divides the accuracy of target detection into grades, and uses the cloud model to transform the detector information into decision matrix to construct the mass function. Then the conflict parameter is introduced and the fusion algorithm is improved to modify and fuse the evidence, achieving accurate decision-making for multi-sensor target detection. Weighted fusion preserves the target accurately detected in a single image, and performs weighted fusion on the same target appearing in both visible and infrared images. Finally, all detection results are merged to achieve fast target detection by decision-level fusion.
3. METHOD
In order to obtain accurate information of the detection target, We firstly selects the camera and the 125-line M1 solid-state LIDAR to collect the campus environment, and synchronize the two sensors in time and space to ensure that they can accurately reflect the environmental state at the same time. Then the two-dimensional image acquired by the camera is recognised using deep learning technology, the network model based on deep learning technology is divided into faster single-stage model and higher accuracy two-stage model, in order to achieve a balance between speed and accuracy, We adopts the single-stage YOLOv5 model. The point cloud obtained by M1 solid-state LIDAR is processed to realize point cloud downsampling and ground point cloud removal. After obtaining the 2D detection frame of the image by YOLOv5 algorithm, the radar point cloud is cut according to the 2D information in the detection frame and the density-based DBSCAN clustering algorithm is used for pedestrian clustering operation, so as to obtain 3D information. Finally, the 2D information acquired by the camera and the 3D information acquired by the LIDAR are fused by decision level fusion, and finally the fused pedestrian information is obtained. Figure 1 shows the technology roadmap.
