3.2.2. Point cloud Clustering based on DBSCAN
In order to identify and distinguish each independent target, it is necessary to cluster the non-ground point cloud to ensure that the points belonging to the same target can be clustered together. In this paper, the DBSCAN clustering algorithm is used to cluster the point cloud targets. DBSCAN is a density-based clustering algorithm. Compared with the traditional K-means algorithm, the DBSCAN clustering algorithm can find clusters of arbitrary shapes and does not need to input the number of categories K. The performance of the proposed algorithm mainly depends on two key parameters: the search radius eps and the density threshold Minpts. Eps is used to define the feature distance between points, and Minpts is used to specify the minimum number of data points required within a given search radius. The selection of these two parameters will directly determine the final effect of clustering. DBSCAN algorithm can effectively identify the clustering structure in the data, and can eliminate the shape limit of the data and the interference of noise points. It has good stability and reliability, and will not be affected by shape of obstacles. The process of DBSCAN algorithm is shown in Figure 2.
