3.3.2. Data fusion of LIDAR and camera
Since LIDARS with fewer beams provide less information about data features, we chose the 125-wire M1 solid-state LIDAR, which has a number of beams comparable to the highest-beam (128-wire) mechanical LIDAR released globally, as a means of obtaining richer data features. By processing the data of M1 LIDAR and camera, the point cloud clustering results and image detection results of the target are obtained respectively. Then, the spatial transformation relationship obtained by the spatio-temporal synchronization of the two sensors is used to accurately project the point cloud of LIDAR clustering into the image detection results, so as to obtain the two-dimensional detection frame projected on the image by the three-dimensional point cloud frame. The fusion strategy is also designed considering the environmental factors, for example, in the case of poor lighting, if only a single sensor detects the target, the detection result of the single sensor is output, and only when both sensors detect the target, the detection result of the LIDAR is fused with the detection result of the camera at the decision level. We adopts the Intersection over Union (IoU) technique for fusing data from the two sensors. Firstly, we extract the LIDAR point cloud clustering results of the current frame and find the matching image detection results, and then a detection box is selected from the image and all point cloud clustering boxes are calculated for IOU. When the IoU value of the two sensor detection boxes exceeds the threshold set in this paper, it is regarded as the fusion detection result of the same object. Until all point cloud clustering boxes are
