4.2. .Experimental results
In this paper, experiments were conducted using the modified wheelchair trolley depicted in Figure 3 under various lighting conditions on campus. Firstly, the internal parameter of the camera is calibrated to obtain the parameter file of the internal parameter matrix of the camera, and the camera and M1 LIDAR are jointly calibrated by using the parameter file. Figure (c) in Figure 4 shows the
visualization effect of point cloud projection when the pedestrian appears in the calibration screen, and it can be seen from the projection effect that the joint calibration projection is relatively close. Then, point cloud pre-processing is performed by downsampling and filtering out ground points, which reduces data volume, enhances processing efficiency, and minimizes ground point interference, leading to a more accurate and efficient clustering process. Figure 4(a) shows the original point cloud of M1 LIDAR, Figure 4(b) shows the preprocessed point cloud. It can be seen that the preprocessed point cloud is more streamlined than the original point cloud, and the influence of outliers and noise points has also been significantly reduced. In particular, the quality of point cloud has been significantly improved after filtering ground points. In this paper, experiments are conducted under both normal light conditions, as illustrated in Figure 5, and dark light conditions, as depicted in Figure 6. Figure (a) presents the detection results of the visual target detection algorithm on the camera image, with the visualization results indicating that detections can be achieved through the camera sensor under normal light, achieving a detection accuracy of 92%. Under dark light conditions, the camera sensor is unable to ensure the stability of detection, leading to missed detections in certain frames. Figure (b) displays the pedestrian point cloud clustered by the M1 LIDAR, Figure (c) shows the LIDAR clustering box, Figure (d) represents the sensor fusion box, and Figure (e) illustrates the pedestrian information detected through fusion. Figure 8 shows the experimental data of the detection accuracy of each sensor using a wheelchair cart in the campus environment, where the detection rate of the camera sensor is 87.7%, the detection rate of the LIDAR sensor is 80.5%, and the detection rate of the sensor fusion is 85.6%. Although the detection rate of camera sensor is 2.1% higher than that of sensor fusion, the camera sensor is easily affected by light, which leads to missed detection. The missed detection problem of single sensor can be avoided by sensor decision-level fusion.
